{{!
	NavCard.mustache — Blitzy component 1, the floating navigation card, and the carrier of
	components 2 and 3 (the outline and gradient calls to action) and of the mobile
	navigation disclosure.

	Nothing in this comment may contain a closing mustache delimiter. A comment tag ends at
	the first one it meets, and everything after it is compiled as template source, so a tag
	written out in full here would silently reopen the template. That is not a theoretical
	hazard: it was reproduced against this exact compiler configuration. Template data is
	therefore named without braces throughout, the way the sibling partials name it, and
	"the escaping form" means the ordinary double-brace interpolation while "the raw form"
	means the triple-brace one.

	INVOCATION
	----------
	Reached only from skin.mustache, referenced bare and at root context with no enclosing
	section. Every path below is written in full from the root of the template data, so
	pushing a context onto this partial would break all of them at once.

	This file is also the sole invoker of PersonalMenu.mustache, which is itself written
	against root context. An error here therefore orphans that partial, which Gate 9 counts
	as a wiring failure even though the orphaned file would still be correct in isolation.

	TEMPLATE DATA CONSUMED
	----------------------
	Navigation, from MediaWiki\Skin\SkinTemplate::getPortletsTemplateData:

	  data-portlets-sidebar.data-portlets-first
	                      The first sidebar section, which on a default wiki is the
	                      navigation portlet and arrives with its id already spelled
	                      p-navigation. That is not an assumption about the wiki's content:
	                      the sidebar is whatever MediaWiki:Sidebar holds, so the links in
	                      this card are always the ones MediaWiki supplies and never a list
	                      written here. Editing MediaWiki:Sidebar changes the card with no
	                      change to this file, which is the proof that the derivation is
	                      real. The key is null on a wiki with no sidebar at all, and a null
	                      section renders nothing.
	  data-portlets-sidebar.array-portlets-rest
	                      Every remaining sidebar section, in the order MediaWiki built
	                      them, rendered immediately after the first one. Normally this is
	                      the toolbox, whose id is p-tb, plus any further wiki-defined
	                      sections. The order is fixed rather than sorted here, because the
	                      captures of this surface are compared byte for byte between
	                      consecutive runs and a reordering would break that comparison.
	                      Note that search and languages are not in this list: MediaWiki
	                      excludes the first and lifts the second out into its own portlet,
	                      which the skin renders elsewhere.

	Per portlet, the keys used are the same ones PersonalMenu.mustache uses, for the same
	reasons:

	  id                  Element id, emitted verbatim. Re-emitting it unaltered is the
	                      entire mechanism by which the preserved p-navigation selector
	                      survives a complete visual redesign.
	  class               mw-portlet plus mw-portlet followed by the section name, gaining a
	                      trailing emptyPortlet when the section has no items and no hook
	                      content. Appended to the Blitzy class rather than replacing it, so
	                      core's hooks and the empty-state marker both pass through
	                      untouched. Adding a class of the skin's own alongside core's is
	                      explicitly permitted.
	  html-tooltip        Raw attribute string from MediaWiki\Linker\Linker::tooltip. It
	                      already carries its own leading space, so it is emitted with none
	                      in front of it and an absent tooltip leaves no stray whitespace
	                      inside the start tag.
	  html-before-portal  Raw HTML contributed around the list by the SkinAfterPortlet hook.
	  html-after-portal   Empty strings unless an extension has something to add, and
	                      emitted so that such content is never silently discarded.
	  array-items         The list entries. Each carries html-item, the complete list item
	                      MediaWiki built, including its id, its mw-list-item class and its
	                      active class when the entry is current. Emitting html-item is what
	                      guarantees every entry MediaWiki supplies reaches the page:
	                      nothing here filters, reorders or curates.

	The sibling key html-items is deliberately not consumed. It is the same entries
	pre-concatenated, so emitting both would render every link twice.

	Logo, from MediaWiki\Skin\Components\SkinComponentLogo. Every key is optional, which is
	why each one is guarded:

	  link-mainpage       Target of the logo link. Supplied by core on every page.
	  data-logos.1x       Path to the square mark. Read only as a presence test, never
	                      emitted: the mark is painted by core, not by this file. See the
	                      note on the mark below for why, and why this is the right key to
	                      test rather than data-logos.icon.
	  data-logos.wordmark An object with src, width and height. Its fourth key, style, is
	                      deliberately NOT emitted: a style attribute is forbidden here and
	                      the stylesheet owns every dimension. width and height are emitted
	                      because MediaWiki requires them on this object and they carry the
	                      intrinsic aspect ratio.
	  msg-mainpage        Text of the wordmark fallback and alternative text of the wordmark
	                      image. A core message, reused rather than reinvented, and accurate
	                      because it names exactly where the link goes.
	  msg-tooltip-p-logo  Advisory title on the logo link. Also a core message.

	The wordmark is the case that actually renders on the verification instance. Its site
	configuration wires a mark and no wordmark, because the Blitzy wordmark is typographic
	and set in the display font from the stylesheet, so the fallback branch below is the one
	photographed in all twenty-one captures. This is the required posture: a missing logo
	must never stall a build, so the wordmark degrades to text at navigation scale and the
	fallback is reported as unresolved in DELIVERY.md.

	THE MARK IS NOT AN IMAGE ELEMENT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
	--------------------------------------------------------
	Declaring the logo feature in skin.json makes core emit a background-image rule for the
	class mw-wiki-logo, built from the 1x logo and pointing at the CACHE-BUSTED url -- the
	same url core also emits as a preload hint. An image element sourced from the raw logo
	path is a DIFFERENT url, so the preload is never consumed and the file is fetched twice.
	That was measured in a browser against this very markup: two requests for one 2 KB asset
	and a console warning about an unused preload, on every page of every capture. Rendering
	the mark as an empty element carrying mw-wiki-logo consumes the preloaded, versioned url
	instead, so the asset is fetched once, and it also means the declared logo feature is
	actually in effect rather than delivered and unused.

	It is also the mechanism the rest of the package already documents: the site
	configuration template states that the logo is wired only through the logo setting and
	is never embedded in a template, and the committed mark says the same in its own
	description. This file is the only place that could have contradicted them.

	The presence test is on 1x rather than on icon because 1x is the key core actually paints
	from. Testing icon would emit an element core had no rule for, leaving an empty box sized
	by the stylesheet with nothing in it. When no logo is configured at all the element is
	simply not emitted and the typographic wordmark stands alone.

	Search, from MediaWiki\Skin\Components\SkinComponentSearch. Everything inside the search
	region is core's own raw HTML, emitted rather than rebuilt:

	  data-search-box.form-action
	                      Form target, the wiki's script path.
	  data-search-box.page-title
	                      Value of the hidden title field, the search special page.
	  data-search-box.html-input
	                      The query field. Already carries id searchInput and already has an
	                      accessible name and placeholder from a core message, so no message
	                      of the skin's own is needed and none is added.
	  data-search-box.html-button-search
	                      The go submit, already carrying id searchButton.
	  data-search-box.html-button-search-fallback
	                      The full-text submit, already carrying id mw-searchButton. This is
	                      not decoration. It is the submit path that works when the
	                      client-side search enhancement has not loaded, so the skin's
	                      no-scripting requirement depends on it being emitted.

	Rebuilding any of those three would drop the ids that gadgets and mediawiki.util expect,
	so they are passed through in the raw form and left alone.

	Calls to action, from MediaWiki\Skins\Blitzy\BlitzyViewModel:

	  data-blitzy-cta-primary
	  data-blitzy-cta-secondary
	                      Both always present, each with a label and, when a target could be
	                      resolved, an href. Both values originate in site configuration and
	                      are therefore untrusted; see the escaping note below.
	  label               Always present, possibly empty. Interpolated in the escaping form.
	  href                ABSENT when no target was configured and when the allowlist in
	                      MediaWiki\Skins\Blitzy\BlitzyUrlValidator rejected the one that
	                      was, so every use of it sits inside a section on it. Emitting an
	                      unguarded href would render an empty attribute, which is a link
	                      back to the page it sits on and has no accessible destination.

	The primary call to action is the outline pill and the secondary is the gradient pill,
	in that order, always. Neither borrows the announcement bar's hook and the announcement
	bar does not borrow theirs, so each component's computed-style assertion names exactly
	one element.

	Messages, all of which must be listed in the messages argument of skin.json — that list
	is what creates the keys at all, and a key that is not listed silently interpolates as
	the empty string:

	  msg-navigation-heading     Accessible name of the navigation landmark. Core message,
	                             reused so the landmark is named consistently with every
	                             other skin.
	  msg-blitzy-nav-toggle      Visible text of the disclosure button and fallback
	                             accessible name on its input. Documented in i18n/qqq.json
	                             as deliberately state-neutral, because one control both
	                             opens and closes the menu.
	  msg-mainpage               See the logo notes above.
	  msg-tooltip-p-logo         See the logo notes above.

	FIXED IDENTIFIERS DEFINED HERE
	------------------------------
	  blitzy-nav-checkbox   The disclosure input.
	  blitzy-nav-button     The disclosure button.
	  blitzy-nav-target     The disclosure target, which is the navigation landmark itself.

	All three are literals, never derived, suffixed, hashed or varied between renders.
	resources/skins.blitzy.js/dropdown.js binds its listeners to them and
	tests/qunit/dropdown.test.js dispatches against them, so they have to be predictable;
	and this card appears in every one of the twenty-one byte-compared captures, so nothing
	in it may differ between two renders of the same URL. Because they are literals, this
	partial must be referenced exactly once per page.

	PRESERVED SELECTORS
	-------------------
	Emitted here and asserted by tests/playwright/domContract.spec.ts: p-navigation, from
	the portlet id; p-search and searchform, the conventional ids that gadgets and
	mediawiki.util look for; and searchInput, searchButton and mw-searchButton, which arrive
	inside core's raw HTML and are preserved by not rebuilding it. p-personal comes from
	PersonalMenu.mustache, which this file invokes. Every mw-prefixed class MediaWiki puts
	on a list item passes through untouched for the same reason.

	THE MOBILE DISCLOSURE
	---------------------
	The skin has to stay usable with scripting switched off, so the disclosure is the
	checkbox hack MediaWiki documents in resources/src/mediawiki.page.ready/checkboxHack.js.
	That documentation is the source of the class names and attributes below, and of one
	structural constraint: the input need only be a PRECEDING SIBLING of the button and the
	target. Nothing in MediaWiki binds to these class names on its own — the module exports
	binders for a skin's own script to call — so opening and closing is done entirely by a
	checked-state sibling selector in
	resources/skins.blitzy.styles/components/NavCard.less. Keeping the input, the button and
	the target as immediate siblings, in that order, is what lets that selector be written
	once with a single general-sibling combinator instead of duplicated per relation.

	dropdown.js then layers dismiss-on-outside-click and Enter activation over it and keeps
	the expanded state current, but it is an enhancement: with it absent the control still
	opens, still closes, and every link inside stays reachable by keyboard. Two consequences
	for anything driving this control programmatically, both of which also hold for the user
	menu: target the input rather than the button, because the stylesheet clips the input
	over it and the input is the topmost hit-test target; and Space activates natively while
	Enter does not, because the element really is a checkbox however it announces itself.

	The markup is UNCONDITIONAL. It is present at every viewport, including the widest,
	where the stylesheet simply ignores it. Omitting it above a width would make a fidelity
	assertion fail rather than skip, and would make the document depend on a viewport the
	server cannot see. For the same reason the navigation list is emitted exactly once and
	serves as both the wide-layout list and the disclosure target; a second copy for small
	screens would duplicate every id inside it.

	THE 768 PIXEL BOUNDARY
	----------------------
	Component 1 is viewport-dependent and is asserted at three widths. At 1440 pixels and at
	768 pixels it is the full 76-pixel card; only BELOW 768 pixels does it become the
	disclosure. The threshold is exclusive, so the stylesheet expresses it as a maximum of
	767 pixels and the card is still in its wide configuration at exactly 768. Of the three
	viewport-dependent components only component 5, the hero type, changes value at that
	width. No width assumption is baked into this markup, which is what leaves the
	stylesheet free to say that.

	Components 2 and 3 are NOT viewport-dependent and must measure identically at all three
	widths, which is why both calls to action sit outside the disclosure target: inside it
	they would be hidden at the narrowest width and their computed values would no longer
	match.

	TEMPLATE DIALECT TRAPS
	----------------------
	Two behaviours of MediaWiki's mustache compiler shape the markup below, and both were
	verified by rendering probes against its exact compile flags rather than assumed.

	First, from inside a section whose value is a plain string, the context stack skips the
	object that string came from and only root-level keys remain reachable. Inside a section
	on href, therefore, neither href nor label resolves; the sibling label comes back empty
	and href itself comes back empty, which would produce a link with an empty target and no
	text at all. The fix, and the shape used below, is to read the string as the implicit
	current value inside its own section and to interpolate the label OUTSIDE that section,
	splitting the anchor's start and end tags across the guard. This is also why sections
	are never nested on two keys of the same object.

	Second, the empty string is TRUTHY in a section here, unlike an absent key. So a guard
	on label cannot suppress an unlabelled call to action, and the claim in skin.json's
	configuration descriptions that this file renders no anchor for an empty label is not
	expressible in this dialect. What is expressible, and what is done, is that the anchor
	is never rendered without a target, and that the element carrying the fidelity hook is
	always present so no assertion can silently skip. On the verification instance both
	labels are configured, so both calls to action are real, named links in every capture.

	STYLING CONTRACT
	----------------
	This file carries class hooks only. There is no style attribute anywhere, not even
	passed through from the logo data, and no colour value; the 24-pixel inset, the
	76-pixel height, the navigation radius, the soft shadow, the 44-pixel call-to-action
	height, the 1.5-pixel outline and the gradient all resolve in NavCard.less against
	resources/skins.blitzy.tokens/tokens.less. There is no directional attribute either:
	logo-leading and actions-trailing placement is a flip-aware stylesheet concern, so the
	stylesheet stays free to flip for right-to-left even though no right-to-left capture is
	produced. Nothing here animates.

	Three notes for the stylesheet, each a real obligation rather than a preference. The mark
	is an empty element painted by core's background-image rule, and core supplies THAT RULE
	ONLY: no size, no repeat setting and no position. The stylesheet therefore has to give
	the mark a width and a height, a contained background size and a no-repeat setting, or a
	32-pixel asset will tile inside whatever box it is given. The wordmark image, by
	contrast, carries its own intrinsic width and height attributes, so it needs sizing only
	to the extent the design departs from them. And below the disclosure breakpoint the
	disclosure input must be clipped rather than removed from the layout, so it is never
	given a hidden attribute here.

	One further obligation, found by driving this markup in a real browser rather than by
	reading it. AT AND ABOVE THE DISCLOSURE BREAKPOINT THE STYLESHEET MUST TAKE THE INPUT
	OFF THE PAGE ENTIRELY, not merely hide the button. A transparent input left in place
	stays the topmost hit-test target wherever it sits -- measured at 1440 pixels, a click
	aimed at the logo landed on the input instead -- and it also stays in the tab order,
	where it is a focusable control that does nothing at that width. Removing it from the
	rendered box tree above the breakpoint solves both at once, and costs nothing: the
	element is still in the document at every width, which is what the fidelity rule
	requires, and the disclosure it drives has no function at that width anyway.

	Any core-owned class arriving inside core's raw HTML — the search field and its two
	submits in particular — is restyled from skinStyles, never from a stylesheet of the
	skin's own.

	DELIBERATELY ABSENT
	-------------------
	Nothing here branches on who is reading. There is no test of an anonymous flag and no
	reference to user identity, so the anonymous and the logged-in card differ only in the
	entries MediaWiki puts in the user menu, which is what makes the two captures of this
	surface comparable. The view model does resolve the primary target differently by login
	state when configuration supplies none, but that decision lives in PHP and this file
	renders whatever label and target it is handed.

	The card holds a logo, the navigation MediaWiki supplies, search, the user menu, two
	calls to action and one disclosure. There is no language switcher, no theme toggle, no
	sticky variant and no notification affordance, because none of those is in scope.

	No subresource here resolves to a foreign origin: the only image is the one the wiki
	itself serves through its logo configuration, and there is no icon font, no external
	avatar and nothing inlined.
}}
<header class="blitzy-nav-card">
	<div class="blitzy-nav-card__inner">
		{{!
			The logo. One link, to the main page, holding at most a mark and always a
			wordmark. When the wordmark image is configured it supplies the accessible
			name through its alternative text; when it is not, the text fallback supplies
			the same words, so the link is never nameless in either case. The mark is
			decorative in both: it repeats what the wordmark already says, so it is
			hidden from assistive technology rather than described twice.

			The mark is an empty element rather than an image element, so that core's own
			background-image rule paints it from the url it has already asked the browser
			to preload. The long note above explains what that avoids and what it obliges
			the stylesheet to supply.
		}}
		<a class="blitzy-nav-card__logo" href="{{link-mainpage}}" title="{{msg-tooltip-p-logo}}">{{!
			}}{{#data-logos.1x}}<span class="blitzy-nav-card__logo-mark mw-wiki-logo" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{/data-logos.1x}}{{!
			}}{{#data-logos.wordmark}}<img class="blitzy-nav-card__logo-wordmark" src="{{src}}" width="{{width}}" height="{{height}}" alt="{{msg-mainpage}}">{{/data-logos.wordmark}}{{!
			}}{{^data-logos.wordmark}}<span class="blitzy-nav-card__logo-wordmark blitzy-nav-card__logo-wordmark--text">{{msg-mainpage}}</span>{{/data-logos.wordmark}}{{!
		}}</a>
		{{!
			The disclosure input, the disclosure button and the disclosure target follow as
			three immediate siblings, in that order. The input is the real control: it is
			what receives focus, what the keyboard operates and what assistive technology
			announces. A button role, a popup hint and an expanded state together make a
			checkbox behave and announce as a disclosure button, and the expanded state is
			set on the input rather than on the button because the button form has been
			deprecated since MediaWiki 1.38. Its server-rendered value is the closed state,
			which is correct both for a first paint and for a reader with no scripting at
			all, and dropdown.js takes over maintaining it from there.

			The labelling reference points at the button, so the accessible name is the same
			words a sighted reader sees, and the label attribute repeats the intent as a
			fallback for the case where that reference cannot be resolved.

			Three omissions are deliberate. There is no name attribute, so the input is
			never submitted with a surrounding form. There is no tabindex, so the element
			keeps its natural place in the tab order. And there is no hidden attribute, for
			the reason given in the styling contract above.

			Autocomplete is switched off so the browser does not restore the checked state
			on reload or on history navigation. Without that, the menu could come back open
			on a page that was captured closed, which is the one way this markup could make
			a capture unreproducible.
		}}
		<input {{!
			}}type="checkbox" {{!
			}}id="blitzy-nav-checkbox" {{!
			}}class="mw-checkbox-hack-checkbox blitzy-nav-card__toggle-checkbox" {{!
			}}role="button" {{!
			}}aria-haspopup="true" {{!
			}}aria-expanded="false" {{!
			}}aria-labelledby="blitzy-nav-button" {{!
			}}aria-label="{{msg-blitzy-nav-toggle}}" {{!
			}}autocomplete="off">
		{{!
			The button is the visible affordance. It is hidden from assistive technology
			because the input above already exposes the same control and announcing both
			would be a duplicate. It still carries real text: that text is what the
			labelling reference resolves to, and it is what a reader sees and clicks. The
			inner element is a styling hook and nothing more.
		}}
		<label {{!
			}}id="blitzy-nav-button" {{!
			}}class="mw-checkbox-hack-button blitzy-nav-card__toggle" {{!
			}}for="blitzy-nav-checkbox" {{!
			}}aria-hidden="true">{{!
			}}<span class="blitzy-nav-card__toggle-text">{{msg-blitzy-nav-toggle}}</span>{{!
		}}</label>
		{{!
			The navigation landmark, which is also the disclosure target. One element serves
			both roles so that the links exist exactly once in the document.

			Each sidebar section becomes a container carrying core's id and class beside the
			skin's own, then core's before-portal content, then one list of core's own list
			items, then core's after-portal content. The first section is written out
			explicitly and the remainder iterated immediately after it, because they arrive
			as two separate keys rather than one list and because their relative order is
			part of the contract.

			The section headings MediaWiki offers for these portlets are not rendered. The
			landmark above carries the accessible name for the whole navigation, and a
			visible heading inside the card would introduce a heading level that the content
			outline does not want.
		}}
		<nav {{!
			}}id="blitzy-nav-target" {{!
			}}class="mw-checkbox-hack-target blitzy-nav-card__nav" {{!
			}}aria-label="{{msg-navigation-heading}}">
			{{#data-portlets-sidebar.data-portlets-first}}
			<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-nav-card__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}"{{{html-tooltip}}}>{{!
				}}{{{html-before-portal}}}{{!
				}}<ul class="blitzy-nav-card__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{!
				}}{{{html-after-portal}}}{{!
			}}</div>
			{{/data-portlets-sidebar.data-portlets-first}}
			{{#data-portlets-sidebar.array-portlets-rest}}
			<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-nav-card__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}"{{{html-tooltip}}}>{{!
				}}{{{html-before-portal}}}{{!
				}}<ul class="blitzy-nav-card__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{!
				}}{{{html-after-portal}}}{{!
			}}</div>
			{{/data-portlets-sidebar.array-portlets-rest}}
		</nav>
		{{!
			The search region. The landmark and the form keep the ids the rest of MediaWiki
			expects, and everything inside the form is core's own markup: the hidden field
			that aims the query at the search special page, the query field, the go submit
			and the full-text submit. None of the three is rebuilt, so each keeps the id and
			the classes core gave it, and the form submits and returns results with no
			script running at all.
		}}
		{{#data-search-box}}
		<div id="p-search" class="blitzy-nav-card__search" role="search">
			<form id="searchform" class="blitzy-nav-card__search-form" action="{{form-action}}">{{!
				}}<input type="hidden" name="title" value="{{page-title}}">{{!
				}}{{{html-input}}}{{!
				}}{{{html-button-search}}}{{!
				}}{{{html-button-search-fallback}}}{{!
			}}</form>
		</div>
		{{/data-search-box}}
		{{!
			The user menu, referenced bare so that it resolves its own portlet paths from
			the root of the template data. This is the only reference to it anywhere in the
			skin.
		}}
		{{>PersonalMenu}}
		{{!
			The calls to action. Two affordances, always in this order: the outline pill
			first, then the gradient pill.

			Each is rendered as a link when a target survived validation and as a plain
			element when none did, so no empty target is ever emitted. The start and end
			tags are split across that guard, and the label is interpolated between them,
			because a section on the target string cannot see the label beside it. Both
			labels come from site configuration and are interpolated in the escaping form,
			which is the single place either is escaped: the view model deliberately leaves
			them raw, so escaping them a second time would render an injected payload as
			visible text instead of neutralising it. The raw form must never be used on
			either value.
		}}
		<div class="blitzy-nav-card__cta">
			{{#data-blitzy-cta-primary}}{{!
				}}{{#href}}<a class="blitzy-cta blitzy-cta--outline" href="{{.}}">{{/href}}{{!
				}}{{^href}}<span class="blitzy-cta blitzy-cta--outline">{{/href}}{{!
				}}{{label}}{{!
				}}{{#href}}</a>{{/href}}{{^href}}</span>{{/href}}{{!
			}}{{/data-blitzy-cta-primary}}
			{{#data-blitzy-cta-secondary}}{{!
				}}{{#href}}<a class="blitzy-cta blitzy-cta--gradient" href="{{.}}">{{/href}}{{!
				}}{{^href}}<span class="blitzy-cta blitzy-cta--gradient">{{/href}}{{!
				}}{{label}}{{!
				}}{{#href}}</a>{{/href}}{{^href}}</span>{{/href}}{{!
			}}{{/data-blitzy-cta-secondary}}
		</div>
	</div>
</header>
